  "progression": [
    "Year one has 60 EC. Calculus 1 precedes Calculus 2 and supports mechanics; calculus and mechanics underpin later electromagnetism and differential equations. Laboratory Skills precedes the laboratory research project. The first-listed astronomy option assumes school mathematics.",
    "Year two has 60 EC. The first-year mechanics, calculus and algebra support quantum physics and computational methods. Quantum Physics 1 precedes Quantum Physics 2 and underpins From Atoms to Solids. The laboratory courses support the advanced experiments. Biomaterials 1 requires Molecules of Life for BME and Materials Science, neither included in the standard first year, so the next listed Complex Analysis choice is selected. It requires the completed first-year calculus sequence.",
    "Year three contains a 30 EC minor, 15 EC in advanced or professional courses and the 15 EC research project. Nanophysics and Advanced Electrodynamics draw on the prior quantum, matter, electromagnetism and waves work. Completion of years one and two plus the first-semester minor yields 150 EC before the scheduled second-semester project. Appendix IV additionally permits the 135 EC exception for eligible third-year students. No specialisation is inferred from the open minor."
  ]
